[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: r7
diagnosis_text:
The issue is not on R6. The affected component is the OSPF process on R7 (FRR/ospfd), which is not running and has no OSPF configuration loaded. Mechanism: with ospfd down on R7, the R6–R7 adjacency on the 67.0.0.0/8 link cannot form, so R7 originates no LSAs for its loopbacks (17.x.x.x) or its LAN (70.0.0.0/8). Upstream routers, including R1, withdraw those routes. R6 still advertises its own connected 67.0.0.0/8, so R1 may retain reachability to that link via R6, but all R7-owned prefixes vanish.

Localization and evidence:
- On R7: “show ip ospf neighbor” returns “ospfd is not running” and systemctl shows frr.service Active: failed. R7’s running config contains no “router ospf” stanza. Interfaces are up (eth0 67.1.1.2/8, eth1 70.1.1.1/8; link detected: yes).
- On R6: OSPF is healthy and running (router-id 16.1.4.1; neighbors up on eth0 46.1.1.2 and eth1 56.1.1.2). OSPF is enabled on 67.0.0.0/8. No neighbor appears for eth2 (67.1.1.1), isolating the failure to the R6–R7 link’s OSPF adjacency.
- On R1: 17.* and 70.0.0.0/8 are absent; only 67.0.0.0/8 is present via the OSPF domain.

Root cause: FRR/ospfd on R7 is failed/misconfigured (no OSPF config), preventing adjacency on R7 eth0 to R6 eth2.

Corrective fix: Restore FRR on R7: add OSPF config (router ospf; network 17.0.0.0/8 area 1; network 67.0.0.0/8 area 1; network 70.0.0.0/8 area 1; set router-id), then start/enable frr.service. Verify R6–R7 adjacency on R6 eth2/R7 eth0 and confirm 17.* and 70.0.0.0/8 reappear on R1.
=== END ===